from tn import *
def C(ch,*a,**k): TN(10,"maths",ch,*a,**k)
C("relations-and-functions","Asha matched each student in her class to a roll number. \"Each student gets exactly one number,\" said her teacher, \"that is a function.\"",
 ["The Cartesian product A x B is the set of all ordered pairs (a, b) with a in A and b in B. If A has m elements and B has n elements, then A x B has mn elements. A relation from A to B is a subset of A x B.",
  "A function from A to B is a relation in which every element of A has exactly one image in B. A is the domain, B the codomain and the set of actual images the range. The vertical line test checks whether a graph is a function.",
  "Types of functions are one-one, many-one, onto, into and bijection. A bijection is both one-one and onto. The horizontal line test checks for a one-one function. Functions can be shown by an arrow diagram, a table, a set of ordered pairs or a graph, and special functions include the identity, constant and real-valued functions."],
 ["Cartesian product","relation","function","domain and range","one-one function","bijection"],
 ("If n(A) = 3 and n(B) = 4, how many elements are in A x B?",["Use n(A x B) = n(A) x n(B).","3 x 4 = 12.","So A x B has 12 elements."]),
 ("In a function, one element of the domain can have two images.",["Each element has exactly one image.","Otherwise it is only a relation."]),
 ["A x B has mn elements.","A function gives exactly one image.","Bijection is one-one and onto.","Vertical line test checks a function."],
 ["m|If n(A) = 2 and n(B) = 5, n(A x B) is|10|7|25|2 x 5.","t|Every function is a relation.|T|Yes.","m|A function that is both one-one and onto is a|bijection|constant|relation only|Definition.","t|A function can give two images to one element.|F|Exactly one image.","m|The set of all inputs is the|domain|range|codomain only|Inputs.","s|n(A x B) when n(A) = 3, n(B) = 4?|12|3 x 4.","m|The vertical line test checks for a|function|bijection|relation only|Each x has one y.","t|The range is the set of actual images.|T|Yes."])
C("numbers-and-sequences","Meena saw tiles arranged in rows of 2, 4, 6, 8. \"What comes next?\" she asked.",
 ["Euclid's division lemma says that for positive integers a and b there are unique q and r with a = bq + r and 0 <= r < b. It is used in Euclid's division algorithm to find the HCF of two numbers. The fundamental theorem of arithmetic says every composite number is a product of primes in a unique way.",
  "A sequence is a list of numbers in a definite order. An arithmetic progression (AP) has a constant difference d, with the n-th term a + (n - 1)d and sum S(n) = n/2 [2a + (n - 1)d]. A geometric progression (GP) has a constant ratio r, with the n-th term a r^(n-1) and sum a(r^n - 1)/(r - 1).",
  "Some special series are the sum of the first n natural numbers n(n + 1)/2, the sum of their squares and the sum of their cubes, which is the square of the first sum. Modular arithmetic with congruence also appears in this unit."],
 ["Euclid's division lemma","HCF","fundamental theorem of arithmetic","arithmetic progression","geometric progression","sum of n terms"],
 ("Find the 10th term of the AP 2, 5, 8, ...",["Here a = 2 and d = 3.","t10 = 2 + 9 x 3.","t10 = 29."]),
 ("The sum of the first n natural numbers is n^2.",["It is n(n + 1)/2.","n^2 is the sum of the first n odd numbers."]),
 ["a = bq + r with 0 <= r < b.","AP: t(n) = a + (n - 1)d.","GP: t(n) = a r^(n - 1).","1 + 2 + ... + n = n(n + 1)/2."],
 ["m|The n-th term of an AP is|a + (n - 1)d|a r^(n-1)|nd|Standard formula.","t|Every composite number is a product of primes.|T|Yes.","m|The common difference of 3, 7, 11 is|4|3|7|7 - 3.","t|A GP has a common difference.|F|It has a common ratio.","m|1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 equals|15|10|20|n(n + 1)/2.","s|10th term of 2, 5, 8, ...?|29|2 + 9 x 3.","m|In a = bq + r, r satisfies|0 <= r < b|r > b|r = b|Division lemma.","t|The 2nd term of a GP is ar.|T|Yes."])
C("algebra","Raju and Asha together bought 3 pens and 2 books for 70 rupees. \"How do we find each price?\" he asked.",
 ["Simultaneous linear equations in two variables can be solved by substitution, elimination and the graphical method. A pair may have a unique solution, no solution or infinitely many, which we judge from the ratios of the coefficients.",
  "The GCD and LCM of polynomials are found by factorisation, and rational expressions are simplified, added, subtracted, multiplied and divided like fractions. The square root of a polynomial is found by factorisation or by division.",
  "A quadratic equation ax^2 + bx + c = 0 is solved by factorisation, completing the square or the formula x = (-b +/- sqrt(b^2 - 4ac)) / 2a. The discriminant b^2 - 4ac tells the nature of the roots, and the sum and product of the roots are -b/a and c/a. Matrices are also introduced as arrays of numbers."],
 ["simultaneous equations","rational expression","quadratic equation","discriminant","roots","matrix"],
 ("Find the discriminant of x^2 - 5x + 6 = 0.",["a = 1, b = -5, c = 6.","b^2 - 4ac = 25 - 24.","The discriminant is 1."]),
 ("A quadratic equation always has two real roots.",["If the discriminant is negative there are no real roots.","It depends on b^2 - 4ac."]),
 ["Solve by substitution or elimination.","Quadratic formula uses the discriminant.","Sum of roots = -b/a.","Product of roots = c/a."],
 ["m|The discriminant is|b^2 - 4ac|b^2 + 4ac|2a|Standard.","t|If the discriminant is zero, the roots are equal.|T|Yes.","m|The sum of roots of x^2 - 5x + 6 = 0 is|5|6|-5|-b/a.","t|The quadratic formula can be used to solve any quadratic.|T|Yes.","m|The roots of x^2 - 5x + 6 = 0 are|2 and 3|1 and 6|-2 and -3|Factorise.","s|Discriminant of x^2 - 5x + 6?|1|25 - 24.","m|A matrix is an|array of numbers|single number|line|Rows and columns.","t|Elimination is a way to solve simultaneous equations.|T|Yes."])
C("geometry","Meena looked at a photo and its enlarged print. \"The shape is the same, only bigger,\" she said.",
 ["Similar figures have the same shape but not always the same size. The Basic Proportionality Theorem (Thales) says a line parallel to one side of a triangle divides the other two sides in the same ratio. Its converse is also true, and the angle bisector theorem divides the opposite side in the ratio of the other two sides.",
  "Triangles are similar by the AA, SSS and SAS criteria. The ratio of the areas of similar triangles equals the square of the ratio of their corresponding sides. The Pythagoras theorem and its converse are proved with similar triangles.",
  "A tangent touches a circle at one point and is perpendicular to the radius there. Two tangents drawn from an outside point are equal in length, and the tangent-chord and alternate segment theorems follow. Constructions of tangents and of triangles with given conditions use a ruler and a compass."],
 ["similar triangles","Basic Proportionality Theorem","AA criterion","tangent","Pythagoras theorem","alternate segment theorem"],
 ("In a right triangle the legs are 3 and 4. Find the hypotenuse.",["Use Pythagoras: c^2 = 3^2 + 4^2.","9 + 16 = 25.","c = 5."]),
 ("Similar triangles always have equal areas.",["They have the same shape, not always the same size.","Area ratio is the square of the side ratio."]),
 ["Thales: a parallel line divides sides proportionally.","AA is enough for similarity.","Tangents from a point are equal.","Tangent is perpendicular to the radius."],
 ["m|The hypotenuse of a 3-4 right triangle is|5|7|6|3^2 + 4^2 = 25.","t|Tangents from an outside point are equal.|T|Yes.","m|The tangent is perpendicular to the|radius at the point of contact|chord|diameter only|Property.","t|Similar triangles have equal sides.|F|Equal angles, proportional sides.","m|The ratio of areas of similar triangles is|square of the ratio of sides|ratio of sides|cube|Theorem.","s|Hypotenuse of a triangle with legs 3 and 4?|5|sqrt(25).","m|A tangent touches the circle at|one point|two points|no point|Definition.","t|AA is a similarity criterion.|T|Yes."])
C("coordinate-geometry","Raju plotted three points on his graph sheet. \"Do they make a triangle or lie on one line?\" he wondered.",
 ["The area of a triangle with vertices (x1, y1), (x2, y2), (x3, y3) is half the absolute value of x1(y2 - y3) + x2(y3 - y1) + x3(y1 - y2). If this area is zero, the three points are collinear. The area of a quadrilateral is found by splitting it into two triangles.",
  "The inclination of a line is the angle it makes with the positive x-axis, and the slope m = tan(theta) = (y2 - y1)/(x2 - x1). Parallel lines have equal slopes and perpendicular lines have slopes whose product is -1.",
  "The equation of a straight line has several forms: slope-intercept y = mx + c, point-slope, two-point, intercept form x/a + y/b = 1 and the general form ax + by + c = 0. The equations of the coordinate axes are y = 0 and x = 0."],
 ["area of a triangle","collinear","slope","inclination","slope-intercept form","general form"],
 ("Find the slope of the line through (1, 2) and (3, 6).",["m = (6 - 2)/(3 - 1).","4 / 2.","The slope is 2."]),
 ("Perpendicular lines have equal slopes.",["Parallel lines have equal slopes.","For perpendicular lines the product is -1."]),
 ["Slope = (y2 - y1)/(x2 - x1).","Collinear points give area zero.","y = mx + c.","Perpendicular: m1 m2 = -1."],
 ["m|The slope of a line through (1, 2) and (3, 6) is|2|3|4|4 / 2.","t|Parallel lines have equal slopes.|T|Yes.","m|The slope-intercept form is|y = mx + c|x/a + y/b = 1|ax + by = 0|Standard.","t|Collinear points make a triangle of positive area.|F|The area is zero.","m|Perpendicular lines have slopes with product|-1|1|0|Property.","s|Slope of a horizontal line?|0|No rise.","m|The equation of the x-axis is|y = 0|x = 0|y = x|On it y is 0.","t|Slope equals tan of the inclination.|T|Yes."])
C("trigonometry","Asha wanted to find the height of a tall tree without climbing it. \"Use the angle of elevation,\" said her teacher.",
 ["Trigonometric identities hold for all angles: sin^2 A + cos^2 A = 1, 1 + tan^2 A = sec^2 A and 1 + cot^2 A = cosec^2 A. They help to simplify expressions and to prove other relations.",
  "In heights and distances, the line of sight is the line from the eye to the object. The angle of elevation is the angle above the horizontal when we look up, and the angle of depression is the angle below it when we look down. The two are equal by alternate angles.",
  "To solve such problems, draw a neat figure, mark the right-angled triangles and use tan, sin or cos as needed. Problems may involve one angle of elevation or depression, or both together."],
 ["trigonometric identity","line of sight","angle of elevation","angle of depression","tan","heights and distances"],
 ("From a point 10 m from a tower the angle of elevation is 45 degrees. Find the height.",["tan 45 = height / 10.","tan 45 = 1.","The height is 10 m."]),
 ("sin^2 A + cos^2 A = 2.",["It equals 1.","This is a basic identity."]),
 ["sin^2 A + cos^2 A = 1.","Elevation is above the horizontal.","Depression is below the horizontal.","Draw a figure first."],
 ["m|sin^2 A + cos^2 A equals|1|0|2|Basic identity.","t|1 + tan^2 A = sec^2 A.|T|Yes.","m|The angle looking up from the horizontal is the angle of|elevation|depression|incidence|Definition.","t|The angle of depression is measured above the horizontal.|F|Below it.","m|tan 45 equals|1|0|sqrt(3)|From the table.","s|Height of a tower seen at 45 degrees from 10 m away?|10|tan 45 = 1.","m|The line from the eye to the object is the|line of sight|tangent|chord|Definition.","t|A neat figure helps to solve problems.|T|Yes."])
C("mensuration","Raju saw a water tank shaped like a cylinder with a half ball on top. \"How much water does it hold?\" he asked.",
 ["The curved surface area of a right circular cylinder is 2 pi r h, its total surface area is 2 pi r (h + r) and its volume is pi r^2 h. A hollow cylinder has an outer and an inner radius, and the material used is found by subtracting the inner volume from the outer.",
  "A right circular cone has curved surface area pi r l where l = sqrt(r^2 + h^2), and volume one third pi r^2 h. A sphere has surface area 4 pi r^2 and volume four-thirds pi r^3. A hemisphere has curved surface area 2 pi r^2, total surface area 3 pi r^2 and volume two-thirds pi r^3.",
  "A frustum is a cone with its top cut off by a plane parallel to the base. Combined solids, such as a cylinder with a hemisphere, are handled by adding or subtracting the individual surface areas or volumes."],
 ["cylinder","cone","sphere","hemisphere","frustum","combined solids"],
 ("Find the volume of a cylinder with r = 7 cm and h = 10 cm (pi = 22/7).",["V = pi r^2 h.","22/7 x 49 x 10.","V = 1540 cubic cm."]),
 ("The volume of a sphere is 4 pi r^2.",["That is its surface area.","The volume is four-thirds pi r^3."]),
 ["Cylinder volume = pi r^2 h.","Cone volume = one third pi r^2 h.","Sphere area = 4 pi r^2.","Frustum is a cone cut by a parallel plane."],
 ["m|The volume of a cylinder is|pi r^2 h|2 pi r h|pi r l|Standard.","t|The surface area of a sphere is 4 pi r^2.|T|Yes.","m|The volume of a cone is|one third pi r^2 h|pi r^2 h|2 pi r h|One third of the cylinder.","t|A hemisphere is half a sphere.|T|Yes.","m|The curved surface area of a cylinder is|2 pi r h|pi r^2 h|4 pi r^2|Standard.","s|Slant height l of a cone is sqrt of r^2 plus what?|h^2|l^2 = r^2 + h^2.","m|A frustum is a|cut-off cone|full sphere|cylinder|Parallel cut.","t|Combined solids are solved by adding or subtracting parts.|T|Yes."])
C("statistics-and-probability","Meena compared the marks of two classmates who had the same average. \"Who is more consistent?\" she wondered.",
 ["Measures of dispersion show how far the data is spread. The range is the largest minus the smallest value. The variance is the mean of the squares of the deviations from the mean, and the standard deviation is its square root, so it has the same unit as the data.",
  "The coefficient of variation CV = (standard deviation / mean) x 100 percent compares the consistency of two data sets. The set with the smaller CV is more consistent.",
  "In probability, we use the sample space and events, with P(E) = favourable / total. The addition theorem says P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A and B), and for mutually exclusive events P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B). Venn diagrams help in these problems."],
 ["dispersion","range","variance","standard deviation","coefficient of variation","addition theorem"],
 ("Find the range of 5, 9, 12, 20.",["Range = largest - smallest.","20 - 5.","The range is 15."]),
 ("A larger coefficient of variation means more consistency.",["A smaller CV means more consistent data.","A larger CV means more spread."]),
 ["Range = largest - smallest.","SD is the square root of the variance.","CV = SD / mean x 100.","P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A and B)."],
 ["m|The range of 5, 9, 12, 20 is|15|25|10|20 - 5.","t|Standard deviation is the square root of the variance.|T|Yes.","m|The more consistent data has the|smaller CV|larger CV|larger range always|Less spread.","t|Variance has the same unit as the data.|F|The SD has the same unit.","m|P(A or B) for exclusive events is|P(A) + P(B)|P(A) x P(B)|P(A) - P(B)|No overlap.","s|Range of 5, 9, 12, 20?|15|20 - 5.","m|CV is|SD / mean x 100|mean / SD|SD x mean|Standard.","t|Probability lies between 0 and 1.|T|Yes."])
